space=0.3, hspace=0.7)\n","\n","for i, (col, color) in enumerate(zip(cols, colors)):\n","    data = df[col].dropna()\n","    stats = data.describe()\n","\n","    # Histogram\n","    ax_hist = fig.add_subplot(gs[i, 0])\n","    ax_hist.hist(data, bins=80, color=color, alpha=0.75)\n","    ax_hist.set_title(f'Histogram of {col}', fontsize=16)\n","    ax_hist.set_xlabel('Price', fontsize=12)\n","    ax_hist.set_ylabel('Frequency', fontsize=12)\n","    ax_hist.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.4)\n","\n","    # Boxplot (horizontal)\n","    ax_box = fig.add_subplot(gs[i, 1])\n","    box = ax_box.boxplot(data, vert=False, patch_artist=True,\n","                        boxprops=dict(facecolor=color, color=color),\n","                        medianprops=dict(color='yellow', linewidth=2),\n","                        whiskerprops=dict(color=color),\n","                        capprops=dict(color=color),\n","                        flierprops=dict(markerfacecolor=color, marker='o', markersize=1, linestyle='none', markeredgecolor='none'))\n","    ax_box.set_title(f'Boxplot of {col}', fontsize=16)\n","    ax_box.set_xlabel('Price', fontsize=12)\n","    ax_box.set_yticks([])\n","    ax_box.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.4)\n","\n","    # Stats text box\n","    ax_text = fig.add_subplot(gs[i, 2])\n","    ax_text.axis('off')\n","    stats_text = (\n","        f\"Count: {int(stats['count'])}\\n\"\n","        f\"Mean: {stats['mean']:.4f}\\n\"\n","        f\"Std: {stats['std']:.4f}\\n\"\n","        f\"Min: {stats['min']:.4f}\\n\"\n","        f\"25%: {stats['25%']:.4f}\\n\"\n","        f\"50% (Median): {stats['50%']:.4f}\\n\"\n","        f\"75%: {stats['75%']:.4f}\\n\"\n","        f\"Max: {stats['max']:.4f}\"\n","    )\n","    ax_text.text(0, 0.5, stats_text, fontsize=14, va='center', ha='left', fontfamily='monospace')\n","\n","plt.suptitle('Histograms, Boxplots, and Statistical Summaries of Close and EMAs', fontsize=22, y=0.93)\n","plt.tight_layout(rect=[0, 0, 1, 0.9])\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"a3c877ef","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.166723,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:53.93416","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:53.767437","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff;line-height:25px\">\n","    \n","The **Exponential Moving Average (EMA)** differentiates itself by applying exponentially decreasing weights to past price data. This weighting scheme places significantly more emphasis on recent prices, enabling the EMA to react more swiftly and sensitively to the latest market movements. As a result, the EMA captures short-term price fluctuations and emerging trends more effectively, making it particularly advantageous for traders focusing on timely entries and exits.\n","\n","In contrast, the **Simple Moving Average (SMA)** calculates an unweighted arithmetic mean of prices over a specified time window, assigning equal importance to all data points within that period. This equal weighting results in a smoother curve, which effectively filters out short-term noise and random volatility. However, this smoothing effect also introduces a lag relative to current price action, meaning the SMA reacts more slowly to abrupt market changes.\n","\n","The inherently higher responsiveness of the EMA makes it more suitable for short-term trend detection and momentum analysis, where capturing recent price dynamics promptly is critical. Meanwhile, the SMA’s smoothing characteristics are often favored for identifying longer-term trends and structural shifts in the market. Both indicators serve as complementary analytical tools, providing nuanced insights into price behavior and assisting investors in making well-informed decisions based on different temporal perspectives."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"0c1a45b0","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.168433,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:54.272011","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:54.103578","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<a id=\"macd\"></a>\n","\n","<h1 style=\" \n","    background-color:#cddcdf; \n","    background-position: center;\n","    font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\n","    font-size: 1.5em;\n","    color: Black;\n","    text-align: center;\n","    padding: 15px;\n","    border-radius: 5px 10px;\n","\">\n","    Moving Average Convergence Divergence Analysis\n","</h1>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"cc3330a9","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.174917,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:54.614064","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:54.439147","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff;line-height:25px\">\n","\n","**Moving Average Convergence Divergence (MACD): A Momentum-Based Trend Oscillator**\n","\n","The **MACD** is a widely adopted momentum oscillator that quantifies the difference between two exponential moving averages (EMAs) of an asset’s price, typically used to evaluate both the **direction** and **strength** of a prevailing trend. By capturing shifts in the rate of price change, the MACD serves as a dynamic tool for detecting **trend continuations**, **potential reversals**, and **momentum divergences** in financial time series data.\n","\n","In standard implementation, the MACD is derived from the subtraction of the **26-day EMA** from the **12-day EMA** of the closing price:\n","\n","$$\n","\\text{MACD} = \\text{EMA}_{12} - \\text{EMA}_{26}\n","$$\n","\n","* **EMA₁₂**: Short-term exponential moving average (faster response to recent price action)\n","* **EMA₂₆**: Longer-term exponential moving average (slower, smoother trend component)\n","\n","To enhance interpretability, a secondary line called the **signal line**—typically a **9-day EMA** of the MACD—is overlaid on the MACD plot. This combination facilitates identification of **bullish** and **bearish crossovers**:\n","\n","* A **bullish crossover** occurs when the MACD line crosses above the signal line, suggesting upward momentum.\n","* A **bearish crossover** occurs when the MACD line crosses below the signal line, indicating potential downward pressure.\n","\n","Additionally, **divergence** between MACD and price movement (e.g., price making higher highs while MACD makes lower highs) may serve as a leading indicator of an impending reversal, offering strategic insight into weakening momentum.\n","\n","In essence, the MACD is a dual-purpose indicator—functioning both as a **trend-following tool** and a **momentum gauge**—that empowers analysts and traders to decode underlying market sentiment with greater precision."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":31,"id":"675c6525","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2025-10-21T10:45:54.991628Z","iopub.status.busy":"2025-10-21T10:45:54.991282Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-10-21T10:45:55.611392Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-10-21T10:45:55.610565Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.801,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:55.614972","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:54.813972","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1800x1200 with 4 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n","\n","df['Date_only'] = pd.to_datetime(df['Date_only'])\n","\n","# Filter date range\n","start_date = pd.Timestamp(\"2025-01-01\")\n","end_date = pd.Timestamp(\"2025-07-31\")\n","df_filtered = df[(df['Date_only'] >= start_date) & (df['Date_only'] <= end_date)].copy()\n","\n","# Clean data\n","df_filtered = df_filtered.replace([np.inf, -np.inf], np.nan).dropna(subset=['Close'])\n","\n","# MACD calculations\n","df_filtered['EMA12'] = df_filtered['Close'].ewm(span=12, adjust=False).mean()\n","df_filtered['EMA26'] = df_filtered['Close'].ewm(span=26, adjust=False).mean()\n","df_filtered['MACD'] = df_filtered['EMA12'] - df_filtered['EMA26']\n","df_filtered['Signal'] = df_filtered['MACD'].ewm(span=9, adjust=False).mean()\n","\n","# Detect crossovers\n","df_filtered['Crossover'] = np.where(\n","    (df_filtered['MACD'] > df_filtered['Signal']) & (df_filtered['MACD'].shift(1) <= df_filtered['Signal'].shift(1)), 'Bullish',\n","    np.where((df_filtered['MACD'] < df_filtered['Signal']) & (df_filtered['MACD'].shift(1) >= df_filtered['Signal'].shift(1)), 'Bearish', None)\n",")\n","\n","# Create figure and GridSpec layout (3 rows, 2 columns)\n","fig = plt.figure(figsize=(18, 12))\n","gs = GridSpec(3, 2, figure=fig, width_ratios=[3, 1], height_ratios=[2, 1, 1], wspace=0.3, hspace=0.4)\n","\n","fig.suptitle('MACD Analysis & Close Price Distribution (Jan–Jul 2025)', fontsize=16)\n","\n","# MACD plot (top row, both columns)\n","ax_macd = fig.add_subplot(gs[0, :])\n","ax_macd.plot(df_filtered['Date_only'], df_filtered['MACD'], label='MACD', color='#2a9d8f', linewidth=2)\n","ax_macd.plot(df_filtered['Date_only'], df_filtered['Signal'], label='Signal Line', color='#f4a261', linestyle='--', linewidth=2)\n","ax_macd.axhline(0, color='gray', linestyle='--', linewidth=1, alpha=0.6)\n","\n","bullish = df_filtered[df_filtered['Crossover'] == 'Bullish']\n","bearish = df_filtered[df_filtered['Crossover'] == 'Bearish']\n","ax_macd.scatter(bullish['Date_only'], bullish['MACD'], color='green', marker='^', s=100, label='Bullish Crossover')\n","ax_macd.scatter(bearish['Date_only'], bearish['MACD'], color='red', marker='v', s=100, label='Bearish Crossover')\n","\n","ax_macd.set_title('MACD & Signal Line with Crossovers', fontsize=14)\n","ax_macd.set_ylabel('MACD Value')\n","ax_macd.legend()\n","ax_macd.grid(alpha=0.3)\n","\n","# Histogram (middle row, left)\n","ax_hist = fig.add_subplot(gs[1, 0])\n","ax_hist.hist(df_filtered['Close'], bins=30, color='#2a9d8f', edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\n","ax_hist.set_title('Histogram of Close Prices', fontsize=14)\n","ax_hist.set_xlabel('Close Price')\n","ax_hist.set_ylabel('Frequency')\n","ax_hist.grid(alpha=0.3)\n","\n","# Boxplot (bottom row, left)\n","ax_box = fig.add_subplot(gs[2, 0])\n","boxprops = dict(facecolor='#f4a261', color='black')\n","medianprops = dict(color='black')\n","whiskerprops = dict(color='black')\n","capprops = dict(color='black')\n","\n","ax_box.boxplot(df_filtered['Close'], vert=False, patch_artist=True,\n","               boxprops=boxprops, medianprops=medianprops,\n","               whiskerprops=whiskerprops, capprops=capprops)\n","ax_box.set_title('Boxplot of Close Prices', fontsize=14)\n","ax_box.set_xlabel('Close Price')\n","ax_box.set_yticks([])\n","ax_box.grid(alpha=0.3)\n","\n","# Stats text box (middle + bottom rows, right column)\n","ax_stats = fig.add_subplot(gs[1:, 1])\n","ax_stats.axis('off')\n","\n","desc = df_filtered['Close'].describe()\n","stats_text = (\n","    f\"Count: {int(desc['count'])}\\n\"\n","    f\"Mean: {desc['mean']:.4f}\\n\"\n","    f\"Std: {desc['std']:.4f}\\n\"\n","    f\"Min: {desc['min']:.4f}\\n\"\n","    f\"25%: {desc['25%']:.4f}\\n\"\n","    f\"50% (Median): {desc['50%']:.4f}\\n\"\n","    f\"75%: {desc['75%']:.4f}\\n\"\n","    f\"Max: {desc['max']:.4f}\"\n",")\n","ax_stats.text(0, 0.5, stats_text, fontsize=12, va='center', ha='left', fontfamily='monospace')\n","\n","plt.tight_layout(rect=[0, 0, 1, 0.95])\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"9626006f","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.16778,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:55.959247","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:55.791467","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff;line-height:25px\">\n","\n","**Technical Insights on MACD Indicator:**\n","\n","1. **Position Relative to the Zero Line:**\n","   When the MACD line is positioned above the zero line, it signifies that the short-term Exponential Moving Average (EMA) — typically calculated over 12 periods — is greater than the long-term EMA, usually computed over 26 periods. This condition reflects positive momentum and is generally interpreted as a bullish signal, indicating upward price pressure and increasing buying interest in the asset.\n","\n","2. **Below the Zero Line Dynamics:**\n","   Conversely, when the MACD line falls below the zero line, it indicates that the long-term EMA has surpassed the short-term EMA, signaling negative momentum. This bearish scenario suggests downward price pressure and heightened selling activity, often interpreted as a warning of potential declines or a continuation of a downtrend.\n","\n","3. **Significance of Crossovers:**\n","   Crossovers between the MACD line and its Signal Line (usually a 9-period EMA of the MACD) are critical for identifying possible shifts in market momentum. A bullish crossover occurs when the MACD line crosses above the Signal Line, potentially signaling a favorable entry point or buy signal for traders. Conversely, a bearish crossover happens when the MACD dips below the Signal Line, which may serve as a sell signal or indicate an opportunity to exit long positions.\n","\n","4. **Volatility Reflected by MACD Oscillations:**\n","   The amplitude and frequency of MACD fluctuations provide insights into market volatility. Larger and more rapid oscillations reflect heightened price volatility and more dynamic market conditions, which traders may interpret as increased risk or trading opportunities depending on their strategy.\n","\n","Overall, this MACD visualization functions as a comprehensive tool for technical analysts and traders by highlighting trend reversals, momentum changes, and aiding in the timing of trade entries and exits. It facilitates a deeper understanding of price dynamics beyond simple moving averages, making it invaluable for making informed trading decisions."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ea4bb553","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.174594,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:56.308121","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:56.133527","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<a id=\"rsi\"></a>\n","\n","<h1 style=\" \n","    background-color:#cddcdf; \n","    background-position: center;\n","    font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\n","    font-size: 1.5em;\n","    color: Black;\n","    text-align: center;\n","    padding: 15px;\n","    border-radius: 5px 10px;\n","\">\n","    Relative Strength Index Analysis\n","</h1>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"927fff61","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.176234,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:56.658252","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:56.482018","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff;line-height:25px\">\n","    \n","**Decoding Market Momentum: A Comprehensive Examination of the Relative Strength Index (RSI)**\n","\n","The **Relative Strength Index (RSI)** is a widely utilized momentum oscillator in technical analysis that quantifies the magnitude and velocity of recent price changes to evaluate overbought or oversold conditions in a financial asset. Developed by J. Welles Wilder Jr., RSI transforms price data into a normalized value oscillating between 0 and 100, facilitating intuitive interpretation of momentum extremes.\n","\n","**Mathematical Foundation and Calculation:**\n","\n","The RSI calculation hinges on the concept of **Relative Strength (RS)**, defined as the ratio of average upward price movements (gains) to average downward price movements (losses) over a specified look-back period, commonly 14 periods. Formally, the RSI is expressed as:\n","\n","$$\n","\\text{RSI} = 100 - \\left(\\frac{100}{1 + RS}\\right)\n","$$\n","\n","where\n","\n","$$\n","RS = \\frac{\\text{Average Gain over 14 periods}}{\\text{Average Loss over 14 periods}}\n","$$\n","\n","The smoothing of average gains and losses typically employs a Wilder’s smoothing technique, a modified moving average, to reduce volatility and noise in the oscillator.\n","\n","\n","**Advanced Interpretations and Market Insights**\n","\n","* **RSI > 70 (Overbought Condition):**\n","  An RSI reading above 70 signals that the asset may have experienced an excessive upward price move in a short time frame, indicating that buying momentum could be unsustainably high. This often precedes a **potential price reversal or corrective pullback**, as market participants may begin to take profits or sellers enter the market. However, in strong trending markets, RSI can remain above 70 for extended periods, so context and confirmation from other indicators or price action are critical.\n","\n","* **RSI < 30 (Oversold Condition):**\n","  Conversely, an RSI value below 30 suggests the asset has undergone substantial downward pressure, potentially indicating it is **oversold**. This condition can presage a **price rebound or reversal to the upside**, driven by bargain hunters or short-covering traders. Similar to overbought signals, sustained bearish trends may keep RSI in the oversold region, so traders often seek confirmation before acting.\n","\n","* **Midpoint Level 50 – Momentum Gauge:**\n","  The 50-level serves as a critical threshold distinguishing **bullish momentum** (RSI > 50) from **bearish momentum** (RSI < 50). Movements crossing this midpoint can signal shifts in the underlying trend strength and direction. For example, an upward crossing through 50 may affirm increasing buying pressure, while a downward breach may validate emerging selling dominance.\n","\n","**Strategic Applications in Trading:**\n","\n","The RSI oscillator provides a **robust framework for traders and analysts** to gauge market momentum dynamics, enabling:\n","\n","* **Identification of Potential Entry and Exit Points:** Traders exploit RSI extremes and midpoint crossovers to time trades, entering near oversold levels or bullish momentum confirmation and exiting near overbought conditions or bearish signals.\n","\n","* **Divergence Analysis:** Comparing RSI trends with price movements can reveal **bullish or bearish divergences**, where the RSI forms higher lows while price forms lower lows (bullish divergence), or RSI forms lower highs while price forms higher highs (bearish divergence). Such divergences often precede significant reversals and are a powerful complement to direct RSI readings.\n","\n","* **Risk Management:** By highlighting momentum exhaustion, RSI can help traders avoid chasing overstretched moves and optimize stop-loss placements around momentum shifts.\n","\n","* **Adaptive Use Across Timeframes:** RSI can be calibrated (e.g., shorter or longer look-back periods) for diverse trading styles — from intraday scalping to long-term position trading — preserving its utility across markets and asset classes."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":32,"id":"3c4e618d","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.077774Z","iopub.status.busy":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.077467Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.435715Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.434544Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.592936,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.437888","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:56.844952","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1500x800 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n","\n","df['Date_only'] = pd.to_datetime(df['Date_only'])\n","\n","# Calculate price changes (delta)\n","delta = df['Close'].diff()\n","\n","# Separate gains and losses\n","gain = delta.where(delta > 0, 0)\n","loss = -delta.where(delta < 0, 0)\n","\n","# Calculate Wilder's smoothed moving averages for gains and losses\n","window_length = 14\n","avg_gain = gain.ewm(alpha=1/window_length, adjust=False).mean()\n","avg_loss = loss.ewm(alpha=1/window_length, adjust=False).mean()\n","\n","# Calculate Relative Strength (RS) and RSI\n","rs = avg_gain / avg_loss\n","df['RSI'] = 100 - (100 / (1 + rs))\n","\n","# Fill NaN RSI values with 50 (neutral midpoint)\n","df['RSI'] = df['RSI'].fillna(50)\n","\n","start_date = pd.Timestamp(\"2025-01-01\")\n","end_date = pd.Timestamp(\"2025-07-31\")\n","filtered_df = df[(df['Date_only'] >= start_date) & (df['Date_only'] <= end_date)]\n","\n","# Define custom colors\n","colors = {\n","    'rsi_line': '#264653',     \n","    'overbought': '#90be6d',   \n","    'oversold': '#f94144',      \n","    'midpoint': '#219ebc',      \n","    'grid': '#8ecae6'          \n","}\n","\n","# Create figure and axis\n","fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(15, 8))\n","\n","# Plot RSI line\n","ax.plot(filtered_df['Date_only'], filtered_df['RSI'], color=colors['rsi_line'], linewidth=2.5, label='RSI')\n","\n","# Plot horizontal reference lines for overbought, oversold, and midpoint levels\n","ax.axhline(70, color=colors['overbought'], linestyle='--', linewidth=1.8, label='Overbought (70)')\n","ax.axhline(30, color=colors['oversold'], linestyle='--', linewidth=1.8, label='Oversold (30)')\n","ax.axhline(50, color=colors['midpoint'], linestyle='--', linewidth=1.2, label='Midpoint (50)')\n","\n","# Set chart title and axis labels with black color\n","ax.set_title('Relative Strength Index (RSI) Analysis', fontsize=18, fontweight='normal', color='black')\n","ax.set_xlabel('Date', fontsize=14, fontweight='medium', color='black')\n","ax.set_ylabel('RSI Value', fontsize=14, fontweight='medium', color='black')\n","\n","# Rotate date labels for better readability\n","fig.autofmt_xdate()\n","\n","# Show legend with customized style\n","ax.legend(loc='upper left', fontsize=13, frameon=True, shadow=True, facecolor='white')\n","\n","# Enable grid with custom style\n","ax.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.5, color=colors['grid'])\n","\n","# Optimize layout and display plot\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":33,"id":"c882e50d","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.78693Z","iopub.status.busy":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.786624Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-10-21T10:45:58.327841Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-10-21T10:45:58.326843Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.717653,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:58.331292","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:57.613639","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1500x800 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n","\n","# Calculate SMA7 and SMA30 if not already present\n","if 'SMA7' not in filtered_df.columns:\n","    filtered_df['SMA7'] = filtered_df['Close'].rolling(window=7).mean()\n","if 'SMA30' not in filtered_df.columns:\n","    filtered_df['SMA30'] = filtered_df['Close'].rolling(window=30).mean()\n","\n","# Define colors for each line\n","colors = {\n","    'close': '#023047',\n","    'ema15': '#219ebc',\n","    'sma7': '#fb8500',\n","    'sma15': '#8ecae6',\n","    'sma30': '#ffb703',\n","    'grid': '#d3d3d3'\n","}\n","\n","sns.set_style(\"whitegrid\")\n","\n","fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(15, 8))\n","\n","# Plot lines for Close, EMA15, SMA7, SMA15, and SMA30\n","line_close, = ax.plot(filtered_df['Date_only'], filtered_df['Close'], label='Close', color=colors['close'], linewidth=2)\n","line_ema15, = ax.plot(filtered_df['Date_only'], filtered_df['EMA15'], label='EMA 15', color=colors['ema15'], linestyle='--', linewidth=2)\n","line_sma7, = ax.plot(filtered_df['Date_only'], filtered_df['SMA7'], label='SMA 7', color=colors['sma7'], linestyle='-.', linewidth=2)\n","line_sma15, = ax.plot(filtered_df['Date_only'], filtered_df['SMA15'], label='SMA 15', color=colors['sma15'], linestyle=':', linewidth=2)\n","line_sma30, = ax.plot(filtered_df['Date_only'], filtered_df['SMA30'], label='SMA 30', color=colors['sma30'], linestyle='-', linewidth=2)\n","\n","# Add invisible scatter points on each line to enable mplcursors hover detection\n","scatters = []\n","for col, color in [('Close', colors['close']), ('EMA15', colors['ema15']), ('SMA7', colors['sma7']), ('SMA15', colors['sma15']), ('SMA30', colors['sma30'])]:\n","    sc = ax.scatter(filtered_df['Date_only'], filtered_df[col], color=color, s=15, alpha=0)  # alpha=0 makes points invisible\n","    scatters.append(sc)\n","\n","# Set plot title, labels, legend, and grid\n","ax.set_title('Close Price with EMA and SMA (7, 15, 30 periods)', fontsize=18, color='black', fontweight='normal')\n","ax.set_ylabel('Price', fontsize=14, color='black')\n","ax.legend(loc='upper left', fontsize=12, frameon=True, shadow=True, facecolor='white')\n","ax.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.4, color=colors['grid'])\n","\n","# Format x-axis dates nicely\n","fig.autofmt_xdate()\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","\n","# Activate mplcursors with hover on scatter points\n","cursor = mplcursors.cursor(scatters, hover=True)\n","\n","@cursor.connect(\"add\")\n","def on_add(sel):\n","    x, y = sel.target\n","    # Ensure x is converted to pandas Timestamp for correct formatting\n","    if not isinstance(x, pd.Timestamp):\n","        x = pd.to_datetime(x)\n","    date_str = x.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')\n","    # Identify which scatter line was hovered\n","    idx = scatters.index(sel.artist)\n","    labels = ['Close', 'EMA 15', 'SMA 7', 'SMA 15', 'SMA 30']\n","    label = labels[idx]\n","    # Set annotation text with label, date, and value\n","    sel.annotation.set(text=f\"{label}\\nDate: {date_str}\\nValue: {y:.2f}\")\n","    sel.annotation.get_bbox_patch().set(fc=\"white\", alpha=0.9)\n","\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"cdbe92b2","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.176595,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:58.688892","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:58.512297","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff; line-height:25px\">\n","\n","**Close Price with EMA and SMA:**\n","\n","The Exponential Moving Average over 15 periods (EMA15) is highly responsive to recent price changes, emphasizing the latest data to capture short-term momentum shifts quickly. In contrast, the Simple Moving Averages over 7 (SMA7), 15 (SMA15), and 30 (SMA30) periods provide progressively smoother trend lines by equally weighting past prices, filtering out noise and highlighting medium- to longer-term trends.\n","\n","Crossovers among these moving averages are valuable signals: when a shorter-term average (e.g., EMA15 or SMA7) crosses above a longer-term average (e.g., SMA15 or SMA30), it typically signals bullish momentum and potential buying opportunities. Conversely, downward crossovers suggest bearish momentum and possible sell signals.\n","\n","**Relative Strength Index (RSI):**\n","\n","The RSI measures price momentum on a scale from 0 to 100, identifying overbought conditions above 70 and oversold conditions below 30. The midpoint of 50 indicates neutral momentum. RSI complements moving average analysis by indicating the strength behind price moves, helping traders confirm trends and anticipate reversals.\n","\n","Together, these indicators provide a robust framework for evaluating market trends and momentum, supporting more informed entry and exit decisions in dynamic trading environments."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ba8214b1","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.186069,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:59.059135","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:58.873066","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<a id=\"bb\"></a>\n","\n","<h1 style=\" \n","    background-color:#cddcdf; \n","    background-position: center;\n","    font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\n","    font-size: 1.5em;\n","    color: Black;\n","    text-align: center;\n","    padding: 15px;\n","    border-radius: 5px 10px;\n","\">\n","    Bollinger Bands\n","</h1>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"15768654","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.182455,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:59.42529","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:59.242835","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff; line-height:25px\">\n","\n","**Advanced Volatility Profiling with Bollinger Bands**\n","\n","**Bollinger Bands** represent a sophisticated volatility-based technical indicator widely utilized in financial market analysis to gauge price dispersion and detect potential overbought or oversold scenarios. The indicator is composed of three distinct lines: a central moving average (typically a 20-period Simple Moving Average), flanked by an upper and a lower band that dynamically expand or contract in response to market volatility.\n","\n","**Mathematical Definition:**\n","\n","$$\n","\\text{Upper Band} = \\text{Middle Band} + (k \\times \\sigma)\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","\\text{Lower Band} = \\text{Middle Band} - (k \\times \\sigma)\n","$$\n","\n","where:\n","\n","* $\\text{Middle Band}$ is the SMA over a defined period (commonly 20),\n","* $k$ is the number of standard deviations (usually 2),\n","* $\\sigma$ is the standard deviation of the price over the same period.\n","\n","**Analytical Insights:**\n","\n","* **Upper Band:** Acts as a dynamic resistance level. Price interactions at or above this band often signal overextended bullish momentum and potential reversal or consolidation zones.\n","* **Lower Band:** Functions as a dynamic support level. Price touches or breaks below this band may indicate oversold conditions and a possible bullish rebound.\n","* **Middle Band:** Reflects the prevailing price trend and serves as a baseline for mean reversion strategies.\n","* **Volatility Measurement:** The width between the upper and lower bands directly correlates with market volatility—widening bands indicate heightened volatility and potential breakout scenarios, whereas contracting bands suggest periods of price compression and low volatility.\n","\n","By integrating these elements, Bollinger Bands enable traders to assess not only the direction and strength of price trends but also the likelihood of imminent price reversals or continuation, thereby providing a comprehensive framework for timing entry and exit points with enhanced precision.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":34,"id":"8d05b045","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2025-10-21T10:45:59.792881Z","iopub.status.busy":"2025-10-21T10:45:59.79247Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-10-21T10:46:00.86339Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-10-21T10:46:00.862597Z"},"papermill":{"duration":1.261921,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:00.869081","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:45:59.60716","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1500x800 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n","\n","# Bollinger Bands Parameters\n","boll_window = 15       # Period for SMA\n","std_multiplier = 2     # Number of standard deviations\n","\n","# Ensure Date_only is datetime type\n","df['Date_only'] = pd.to_datetime(df['Date_only'])\n","\n","# --- Bollinger Bands Calculation ---\n","df['Middle_Band'] = df['Close'].rolling(window=boll_window).mean()\n","rolling_std = df['Close'].rolling(window=boll_window).std()\n","\n","df['Upper_Band'] = df['Middle_Band'] + std_multiplier * rolling_std\n","df['Lower_Band'] = df['Middle_Band'] - std_multiplier * rolling_std\n","\n","# Handle potential NaNs at beginning of data\n","df[['Upper_Band', 'Lower_Band']] = df[['Upper_Band', 'Lower_Band']].fillna(df['Close'])\n","\n","# --- Plotting ---\n","sns.set_style(\"whitegrid\")\n","fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(15, 8))\n","\n","# Plot Bollinger Bands and Close Price\n","ax.plot(df['Date_only'], df['Close'], label='Close', color='#1f77b4', linewidth=2)\n","ax.plot(df['Date_only'], df['Middle_Band'], label='Middle Band (SMA-20)', linestyle='--', color='#ff7f0e', linewidth=1.5)\n","ax.plot(df['Date_only'], df['Upper_Band'], label='Upper Band (+2σ)', linestyle='--', color='#2ca02c', linewidth=1.5)\n","ax.plot(df['Date_only'], df['Lower_Band'], label='Lower Band (−2σ)', linestyle='--', color='#d62728', linewidth=1.5)\n","\n","# Customize main plot\n","ax.set_title('Bollinger Bands and Close Price Dynamics', fontsize=18, fontweight='bold', color='#264653')\n","ax.set_xlabel('Date', fontsize=13)\n","ax.set_ylabel('Price', fontsize=13)\n","ax.legend(loc='upper left', fontsize=11, frameon=True)\n","ax.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.4)\n","\n","# --- Inset Plot (Zoomed Region) ---\n","inset_ax = ax.inset_axes([0.12, 0.53, 0.45, 0.35])  # [x0, y0, width, height]\n","\n","# Define zoomed-in date range (first 7 months of 2025)\n","zoom_start = pd.Timestamp(\"2025-01-01\")\n","zoom_end = pd.Timestamp(\"2025-07-31\")\n","zoom_df = df[(df['Date_only'] >= zoom_start) & (df['Date_only'] <= zoom_end)]\n","\n","# Plot zoomed-in data\n","inset_ax.plot(zoom_df['Date_only'], zoom_df['Close'], color='#1f77b4', linewidth=1.5)\n","inset_ax.plot(zoom_df['Date_only'], zoom_df['Middle_Band'], linestyle='--', color='#ff7f0e', linewidth=1.2)\n","inset_ax.plot(zoom_df['Date_only'], zoom_df['Upper_Band'], linestyle='--', color='#2ca02c', linewidth=1.2)\n","inset_ax.plot(zoom_df['Date_only'], zoom_df['Lower_Band'], linestyle='--', color='#d62728', linewidth=1.2)\n","\n","# Customize inset\n","inset_ax.set_xlim(zoom_start, zoom_end)\n","inset_ax.set_ylim(zoom_df['Lower_Band'].min() * 0.98, zoom_df['Upper_Band'].max() * 1.02)\n","inset_ax.set_title(\"Zoom: Jan–Jul 2025\", fontsize=10)\n","inset_ax.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.3)\n","\n","# Draw a box on main plot showing zoomed region\n","ax.indicate_inset_zoom(inset_ax, edgecolor=\"black\", linewidth=1.2)\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"34298276","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.184804,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:01.24359","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:01.058786","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff; line-height:25px\">\n","\n","This visualization illustrates how Bollinger Bands dynamically adapt to market conditions by enveloping the **Close** price within upper and lower volatility thresholds, calculated using a 15-period Simple Moving Average (SMA) and two standard deviations.\n","\n","* The **Upper Band** consistently acts as a **resistance level**, where price movements nearing or breaching this boundary often signal **overbought conditions**. Such interactions may precede a loss in upward momentum or a short-term pullback.\n","* Conversely, the **Lower Band** functions as a **support level**, with price touches or breakdowns below it frequently indicating **oversold conditions**, triggering potential bullish reversals or buying interest.\n","* The **Middle Band** (20-period SMA) offers a reference point for the **short-term trend**, allowing traders to assess whether the market is trending upward, downward, or entering consolidation.\n","\n","**During the zoomed-in period from January to July 2025**:\n","\n","* The **Close** price repeatedly oscillates between the upper and lower bands, indicating a phase of **heightened volatility** and lack of strong directional bias.\n","* Multiple rejections from the **Upper Band** followed by retracements toward the **Middle or Lower Band** highlight short-term **price exhaustion at resistance levels**.\n","* Likewise, rebounds off the **Lower Band** toward the middle zone suggest **buying activity near support**, creating **mean-reversion opportunities**.\n","\n","Overall, this Bollinger Bands overlay serves as a valuable tool to assess **market volatility**, detect **momentum shifts**, and identify **potential entry or exit zones** based on the position of the close price within the bands. The frequent interactions with both outer bands during this period suggest a **range-bound environment**, where short-term strategies such as **band fades** or **breakout anticipation** may be particularly effective."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_co