val) else ''\n","    for val in row\n","] for row in filtered_corr.to_numpy()]\n","\n","# Plot heatmap\n","plt.figure(figsize=(18, 10))\n","heatmap = sns.heatmap(\n","    filtered_corr,\n","    vmin=-1, vmax=1,\n","    annot=annot, fmt='',\n","    cmap=custom_palette,\n","    linewidths=0.5,\n","    square=True,\n","    cbar_kws={\"shrink\": 0.85}\n",")\n","\n","heatmap.set_title('Filtered Correlation Heatmap (≥ 0.25)', fontsize=16, color='black', pad=20)\n","plt.xticks(fontsize=10, rotation=45, ha='right')\n","plt.yticks(fontsize=10, rotation=0)\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"63e2db54","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.187085,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:04.086174","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:03.899089","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<a id=\"mo\"></a>\n","\n","<h1 style=\"\n","    background-color:#264653; \n","    background-size: cover;\n","    background-position: center;\n","    font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\n","    font-size: 1.5em;\n","    color: white;\n","    text-align: center;\n","    padding: 15px;\n","    border-radius: 15px 50px;\n","    border: 1px solid black;\n","\">\n","    Modeling & Forecasting\n","</h1>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"cc9c4188","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.194869,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:04.473929","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:04.27906","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<a id=\"AR\"></a>\n","\n","<h1 style=\" \n","    background-color:#cddcdf; \n","    background-position: center;\n","    font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\n","    font-size: 1.5em;\n","    color: Black;\n","    text-align: center;\n","    padding: 15px;\n","    border-radius: 5px 10px;\n","\">\n","    ARIMA\n","</h1>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"11670d8e","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.222905,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:04.90148","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:04.678575","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff; line-height:25px\">\n","    \n","**A Methodological Approach to Time Series Forecasting: The ARIMA Framework**\n","\n","The provided visual representation, a filtered correlation heatmap, serves as a foundational analytical tool, elucidating the interrelationships among various financial time series variables. Specifically, it quantifies Pearson correlation coefficients that meet or exceed a threshold of 0.25, complemented by indicators of statistical significance (denoted by three asterisks, conventionally signifying a p-value less than 0.001). The array of variables encompasses fundamental price metrics—\"Close,\" \"Open,\" \"High,\" and \"Low\"—along with \"Volume,\" \"Daily_Return,\" and an extensive set of technical indicators: Simple Moving Averages (SMAs) and Exponential Moving Averages (EMAs) across diverse periods (\"SMA3,\" \"SMA7,\" ..., \"SMA50,\" and \"EMA3,\" \"EMA7,\" ..., \"EMA50\"). Furthermore, components of Bollinger Bands, namely \"Middle_Band\" and \"Upper_Band,\" are included, providing insights into market volatility and trend envelopes.\n","\n","The overarching objective of this endeavor is to construct robust predictive models for these financial time series. The planned methodology will systematically progress from established statistical techniques, commencing with ARIMA (AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average) models, to more sophisticated deep learning architectures such as LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory) and Transformer-based models. The initial phase, focusing on ARIMA, is critically informed by the explicit correlation insights derived from the heatmap.\n","\n","**Integrating Correlation Insights into ARIMA Modeling**\n","\n","It is imperative to recognize that the ARIMA model fundamentally operates as a univariate time series framework. Its theoretical underpinnings are rooted in the concepts of **autocorrelation** (the linear dependency of a series on its past values) and **partial autocorrelation** (the correlation between a series and its lagged values, with the influence of intervening lags removed). While the ARIMA model itself does not directly assimilate the cross-correlations observed in the heatmap (e.g., the correlation between 'Close' price and 'SMA3') into its intrinsic structure, these correlational relationships provide indispensable guidance for the judicious selection of the primary series for modeling and, more critically, for the identification of pertinent **exogenous variables** within an ARIMAX (ARIMA with Exogenous Variables) framework.\n","\n","The following strategies outline my approach to leveraging the correlation heatmap's information for the principled development of an ARIMA model:\n","\n","1.  **Selection of the Endogenous (Primary) Series for Forecasting:**\n","    * In financial time series analysis, the \"Close\" price typically constitutes the primary variable of interest for forecasting. The heatmap corroborates its substantial correlations with numerous other indicators, thereby validating its selection as the core series for ARIMA modeling.\n","    * The exceptionally high correlation coefficients (approaching 1.00) observed between the 'Close' price and various SMA/EMA values implicitly highlight the pronounced lagged dependencies inherent within the 'Close' price series itself. These strong internal autocorrelations are precisely what the AutoRegressive (AR) and Moving Average (MA) components of the ARIMA model are designed to capture and model.\n","\n","2.  **Incorporation of Correlated Variables as Exogenous Inputs (ARIMAX Model):**\n","    * While classical ARIMA is univariate, the ARIMAX extension allows for the inclusion of additional independent variables that are hypothesized to exert influence on the target series. This extension represents a crucial avenue for leveraging the cross-correlational insights gleaned from the heatmap.\n","    * **Candidate Exogenous Variables derived from the Heatmap:**\n","        * **'Daily_Return':** This variable demonstrates a notable positive correlation (0.67) with 'Close' and other price-related metrics. As 'Daily_Return' represents the proportional change in price, it often exhibits greater stationarity properties compared to raw price series. Its inclusion as an exogenous variable in an ARIMAX model can account for short-term market dynamics, such as volatility and momentum, which may not be fully explained by the historical 'Close' prices alone.\n","        * **'Volume':** Despite a more moderate correlation (0.68) with 'Close' compared to certain technical indicators, 'Volume' furnishes critical information regarding market activity, liquidity, and conviction behind price movements. Significant price shifts are often accompanied by corresponding changes in volume, rendering it a valuable exogenous predictor for enhancing forecasting precision.\n","        * **SMA/EMA, 'Middle_Band,' and 'Upper_Band':** These technical indicators exhibit extremely high correlations (often approaching 1.00) with the 'Close' price. Direct inclusion of these raw indicator values as exogenous variables could potentially introduce issues related to **multicollinearity**, given their derivation from the 'Close' price series. To mitigate this, a more appropriate strategy involves incorporating their *differences* or *deviations* relative to the 'Close' price (e.g., `(Close - SMA3)`). These transformed variables can effectively capture dynamic relationships, such as crossovers or divergences, providing valuable insights into trend strength, momentum, and potential reversal points without redundant collinearity. The 'Middle_Band' and 'Upper_Band,' in particular, offer context regarding price deviation from moving averages and potential volatility channels, which can be highly informative for forecasting price boundaries or trend continuations.\n","\n","3.  **Determination of the ARIMA Model Order ($p, d, q$):**\n","    * The ARIMA model is parameterized by three integer orders: $p$ for the AutoRegressive component, $d$ for the Integrated (differencing) component, and $q$ for the Moving Average component.\n","    * **$p$ (AutoRegressive Order):** This parameter specifies the number of lagged observations of the series that are included in the model. Significant positive correlations in the **Autocorrelation Function (ACF)** plot of a stationary series are indicative of the presence of AR terms. The high correlations between 'Close' and its various lagged moving averages (SMA/EMA) observed in the heatmap implicitly suggest substantial autoregressive characteristics within the 'Close' price series that warrant modeling through the $p$ parameter.\n","    * **$d$ (Integrated Order - Differencing):** This parameter quantifies the number of non-seasonal differences required to achieve stationarity of the time series. Financial price series are typically non-stationary; their statistical properties (mean, variance, autocorrelation) evolve over time. The inherent stationarity often observed in 'Daily_Return' (which is essentially a first-order logarithmic difference) provides a strong empirical rationale for an initial $d$ value of 1 for the 'Close' price series. Achieving stationarity is a prerequisite for valid statistical inference and stable parameter estimation in ARIMA models.\n","    * **$q$ (Moving Average Order):** This parameter denotes the number of lagged forecast errors that are included in the model. Significant correlations in the **Partial Autocorrelation Function (PACF)** plot of a stationary series are often indicative of the presence of MA terms.\n","\n","**Systematic Implementation Stages for ARIMA Modeling:**\n","\n","1.  **Data Acquisition and Preprocessing:**\n","    * Ensure comprehensive availability of time series data for all variables highlighted in the heatmap.\n","    * Verify that the data is meticulously sorted in chronological order and indexed appropriately.\n","    * Conduct thorough inspection for missing values and implement robust strategies for imputation (e.g., linear interpolation, forward/backward fill) or selective exclusion.\n","\n","2.  **Exploratory Time Series Analysis (for the 'Close' Price):**\n","    * Visualize the 'Close' price series over time to discern overarching trends, potential seasonality (even if not explicitly captured by daily correlations), and volatility patterns.\n","    * Perform formal **stationarity tests**, such as the Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test or the Kwiatkowski-Phillips-Schmidt-Shin (KPSS) test, on the 'Close' series. If non-stationary, apply sequential differencing and re-evaluate stationarity to empirically determine the optimal $d$ order.\n","    * Generate and analyze the **ACF and PACF plots** for the stationary (or differenced) 'Close' price series. These plots are instrumental for proposing initial values for the $p$ and $q$ parameters based on their characteristic decay or cut-off points.\n","\n","3.  **Exogenous Variable Selection for ARIMAX:**\n","    * Based on the empirical evidence from the heatmap, 'Daily_Return' and 'Volume' emerge as highly plausible candidates for exogenous variables.\n","    * Rigorously assess potential **multicollinearity** among the chosen exogenous variables and between them and the primary series, potentially employing metrics like the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF). When high correlation exists for variables like SMA/EMA, consider utilizing their transformed versions (differences or deviations) to mitigate collinearity.\n","    * Evaluate the potential for **interaction terms** between selected exogenous variables, informed by domain expertise, to capture synergistic effects.\n","\n","4.  **Model Estimation (ARIMA/ARIMAX):**\n","    * Utilize robust statistical programming libraries, such as `statsmodels` in Python.\n","    * Fit the ARIMA model using the empirically derived $p, d, q$ orders.\n","    * In the case of an ARIMAX specification, incorporate the pre-selected exogenous variables into the model estimation function (e.g., `exog` parameter in `ARIMA` or `SARIMAX`).\n","\n","5.  **Model Diagnostics and Evaluation:**\n","    * **Residual Analysis:** Conduct a comprehensive analysis of the model's residuals. The residuals should ideally approximate white noise (i.e., exhibit a zero mean, constant variance, and no significant autocorrelation). This is validated through residual ACF/PACF plots, statistical tests (e.g., Ljung-Box test for autocorrelation), and normality plots of the residuals.\n","    * **Information Criteria:** Employ information criteria such as the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) and Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) to objectively compare and select among various ARIMA/ARIMAX model specifications (i.e., different combinations of $p, d, q$ and exogenous variables). Models yielding lower AIC/BIC values are generally preferred.\n","    * **Forecast Performance Assessment:** Partition the dataset into distinct training and testing subsets. Generate out-of-sample forecasts on the test set. Quantify forecast accuracy using widely accepted metrics such as Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE), and Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE).\n","\n","6.  **Iterative Model Refinement and Optimization:**\n","    * Based on the outcomes of the diagnostic and evaluation phases, systematically iterate on model parameters and exogenous variable configurations.\n","    * Consider employing automated parameter search algorithms, such as grid search or `pmdarima.auto_arima`, to efficiently identify optimal $(p, d, q)$ orders, while ensuring the appropriate inclusion of exogenous variables.\n","    * If seasonal patterns are identified (which may manifest on weekly or monthly cycles rather than daily, even if not explicitly highlighted by the initial daily correlation heatmap), extend the modeling framework to a Seasonal ARIMA (SARIMA) model.\n","    * For more robust and realistic evaluation in dynamic financial markets, implement **rolling forecasts** or expanding window forecasts, where the model is periodically re-estimated or retrained with new data.\n","\n","This comprehensive, data-driven methodology ensures a rigorous and effective application of the ARIMA framework, not only by capturing the inherent temporal dependencies within the target series but also by strategically leveraging the external information provided by the correlation heatmap to enhance predictive power."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":38,"id":"32c15937","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2025-10-21T10:46:05.295739Z","iopub.status.busy":"2025-10-21T10:46:05.295456Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-10-21T10:46:09.602336Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-10-21T10:46:09.601286Z"},"papermill":{"duration":4.502237,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:09.603734","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:05.101497","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["\u001b[96mDataFrame prepared for ARIMA modeling. Shape: (4039, 9)\u001b[0m\n","\u001b[96mTime period for modeling: 2014-10-01 00:00:00 to 2025-10-21 00:00:00\u001b[0m\n","\u001b[96mSelected exogenous variables: ['Daily_Return', 'Volume', 'RSI', 'Middle_Band', 'Upper_Band', 'Lower_Band', 'SMA7', 'EMA7']\u001b[0m\n","\n","\u001b[92m--- Step 1: Stationarity Test for 'Close' Price ---\u001b[0m\n","ADF Statistic (Close): -0.0736\n","P-value (Close): 0.9520\n","Critical Values (Close): {'1%': -3.4319830157514812, '5%': -2.86226157702636, '10%': -2.56715410307542}\n","Conclusion: 'Close' series is likely NON-STATIONARY. Attempting first differencing.\n","\n","--- Stationarity Test for Differenced 'Close' Price (d=1) ---\n","ADF Statistic (Differenced Close): -9.4808\n","P-value (Differenced Close): 0.0000\n","Critical Values (Differenced Close): {'1%': -3.4319830157514812, '5%': -2.86226157702636, '10%': -2.56715410307542}\n","Conclusion: Differenced 'Close' series is likely STATIONARY. We will use d=1.\n","\n","Determined 'd' order for ARIMA: 1\n","\n","\u001b[92m--- Step 2: Plotting ACF and PACF for p and q order determination ---\u001b[0m\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1400x600 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1400x600 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["\n","Interpretation Guidance for ACF/PACF:\n","- **ACF (Autocorrelation Function):** Measures the correlation between the series and its lagged values.\n","  - If it dies down slowly, it suggests an AR component. If it cuts off sharply after 'q' lags, it suggests an MA(q) process.\n","- **PACF (Partial Autocorrelation Function):** Measures the correlation between the series and its lagged values, with the influence of intervening lags removed.\n","  - If it dies down slowly, it suggests an MA component. If it cuts off sharply after 'p' lags, it suggests an AR(p) process.\n","\n","Based on these plots, you'd select initial 'p' and 'q' values. For financial time series, patterns can be complex. Often, small values like p=1, q=1 or p=2, q=2 are good starting points.\n","\n","Initial chosen ARIMA orders: p=1, d=1, q=1\n","\n","\u001b[92m--- Step 3: Data Splitting ---\u001b[0m\n","Total data points for modeling: 4039\n","Training data points: 3231 (from 2014-10-01 00:00:00 to 2023-08-05 00:00:00)\n","Testing data points: 808 (from 2023-08-06 00:00:00 to 2025-10-21 00:00:00)\n","\n","\u001b[92m--- Step 4: Fitting ARIMAX Model ---\u001b[0m\n","\n","ARIMAX Model Summary:\n","                               SARIMAX Results                                \n","==============================================================================\n","Dep. Variable:                  Close   No. Observations:                 3231\n","Model:               SARIMAX(1, 1, 1)   Log Likel