y_data"},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1400x600 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1400x600 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["\n","Ljung-Box Test Results for Residuals:\n","       lb_stat      lb_pvalue\n","10  502.838012  1.092524e-101\n","20  556.813034  3.516863e-105\n","30  604.586777  3.324785e-108\n","\n","Interpretation of Ljung-Box Test:\n","- For each lag, if the 'lb_pvalue' is > 0.05, it suggests there's no significant remaining autocorrelation at that lag.\n","- If any 'lb_pvalue' is <= 0.05, it suggests that the residuals are NOT white noise, indicating the model could be improved.\n","\n","\u001b[96m--- Next Steps for ARIMAX Model Improvement (Iterative Refinement) ---\u001b[0m\n","1. **Parameter Optimization (p, d, q):**\n","   - Re-evaluate ACF/PACF of residuals. If there are significant spikes outside the blue confidence bands, adjust `p` and `q` accordingly.\n","   - Use the AIC/BIC values from the `model_fit.summary()`: lower values are better. Try different (p, d, q) combinations.\n","   - Consider using `pmdarima.auto_arima` (a separate library) which automates the search for optimal (p, d, q) based on information criteria.\n","2. **Exogenous Variable Selection:**\n","   - Experiment with different subsets of `existing_exog_variables`. Some variables might not be contributing much or might introduce noise.\n","   - If you detect multicollinearity (e.g., using VIF scores) among your exogenous variables, consider removing highly correlated ones or combining them.\n","   - Explore interaction terms or polynomial terms for exogenous variables if they seem to have non-linear effects.\n","3. **Seasonality (SARIMAX):**\n","   - Although daily Bitcoin prices might not show strong daily/weekly seasonality, if you are modeling other time frequencies (e.g., hourly) or if you suspect yearly cycles for some assets, consider adding `seasonal_order=(P, D, Q, S)` to the `SARIMAX` model.\n","4. **Robust Validation:**\n","   - Implement **Rolling Forecasts** (also known as Walk-Forward Optimization): This is a more robust validation technique for time series where the model is re-trained or updated as new data becomes available. This simulates real-world forecasting more accurately.\n","5. **Error Analysis:**\n","   - Further investigate periods where the model's predictions significantly deviate from actual values. Are there specific market events or conditions during these periods that the model failed to capture?\n","6. **Model Comparison:**\n","   - Once you have a refined ARIMAX model, you can compare its performance (using RMSE, MAE, etc.) against other models like LSTM or Transformer-based models, which you mentioned you want to implement next.\n"]}],"source":["warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n","\n","custom_plot_colors = ['#264653', '#287271', '#2a9d8f', '#e9c46a', '#efb366', '#f4a261', '#ee8951', '#e76f51']\n","\n","# ANSI escape codes for text coloring\n","COLOR_START_TITLE = \"\\033[96m\" # Light Cyan for section titles\n","COLOR_START_STEP = \"\\033[92m\"  # Green for step titles\n","COLOR_END = \"\\033[0m\"         # Reset color\n","\n","# --- Prepare DataFrame for ARIMA modeling ---\n","# Create a copy to avoid modifying the original 'df' for safety,\n","# so 'df' remains untouched for future models like LSTM.\n","df_arima_modeling = df.set_index('Date').copy()\n","\n","# Ensure the index is timezone-naive for consistency with statsmodels.\n","# This explicitly removes timezone information, preventing TypeError in comparisons.\n","if df_arima_modeling.index.tz is not None:\n","    df_arima_modeling.index = df_arima_modeling.index.tz_localize(None)\n","\n","# Drop columns that are not directly used in the model or are redundant.\n","df_arima_modeling = df_arima_modeling.drop(columns=['Date_only', 'DayOfWeek'], errors='ignore')\n","\n","# Identify the target variable\n","target_variable = 'Close'\n","\n","# Identify potential exogenous variables based on correlation discussion from your heatmap analysis.\n","exog_variables_candidates = [\n","    'Daily_Return', 'Volume', 'MACD', 'Signal', 'RSI',\n","    'Middle_Band', 'Upper_Band', 'Lower_Band','SMA7', 'EMA7',\n","]\n","\n","# Filter out any candidate exogenous variables that might not exist in your 'df_arima_modeling'\n","existing_exog_variables = [col for col in exog_variables_candidates if col in df_arima_modeling.columns]\n","\n","# Select only the target and existing exogenous variables for our modeling DataFrame\n","# and drop any rows that contain NaN values in these critical columns.\n","# NaN values arise from initial periods due to rolling window calculations for indicators.\n","df_arima_clean = df_arima_modeling[[target_variable] + existing_exog_variables].dropna()\n","\n","# Separate target and exogenous variables\n","y = df_arima_clean[target_variable]  # Endogenous variable\n","X = df_arima_clean[existing_exog_variables] # Exogenous variables\n","\n","print(f\"{COLOR_START_TITLE}DataFrame prepared for ARIMA modeling. Shape: {df_arima_clean.shape}{COLOR_END}\")\n","print(f\"{COLOR_START_TITLE}Time period for modeling: {df_arima_clean.index.min()} to {df_arima_clean.index.max()}{COLOR_END}\")\n","print(f\"{COLOR_START_TITLE}Selected exogenous variables: {existing_exog_variables}{COLOR_END}\")\n","\n","# --- Step 1: Check for Stationarity of the 'Close' series (Determine 'd' order) ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_STEP}--- Step 1: Stationarity Test for 'Close' Price ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","adf_test_close = adfuller(y)\n","print(f\"ADF Statistic (Close): {adf_test_close[0]:.4f}\")\n","print(f\"P-value (Close): {adf_test_close[1]:.4f}\")\n","print(f\"Critical Values (Close): {adf_test_close[4]}\")\n","\n","d_order = 0 # Default to 0 differencing\n","\n","if adf_test_close[1] > 0.05:\n","    print(\"Conclusion: 'Close' series is likely NON-STATIONARY. Attempting first differencing.\")\n","    d_order = 1 # Set 'd' order to 1\n","    y_diff = y.diff().dropna() # Calculate first difference\n","    adf_test_diff = adfuller(y_diff)\n","    print(\"\\n--- Stationarity Test for Differenced 'Close' Price (d=1) ---\")\n","    print(f\"ADF Statistic (Differenced Close): {adf_test_diff[0]:.4f}\")\n","    print(f\"P-value (Differenced Close): {adf_test_diff[1]:.4f}\")\n","    print(f\"Critical Values (Differenced Close): {adf_test_diff[4]}\")\n","\n","    if adf_test_diff[1] > 0.05:\n","        print(\"Conclusion: Differenced 'Close' series is also likely NON-STATIONARY.\")\n","        print(\"For financial data, d=1 is often sufficient. Consider higher order (d=2) or transformations if issues persist.\")\n","    else:\n","        print(\"Conclusion: Differenced 'Close' series is likely STATIONARY. We will use d=1.\")\n","else:\n","    print(\"Conclusion: 'Close' series is likely STATIONARY. We will use d=0.\")\n","\n","print(f\"\\nDetermined 'd' order for ARIMA: {d_order}\")\n","\n","# --- Step 2: Plot ACF and PACF for determining 'p' and 'q' orders ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_STEP}--- Step 2: Plotting ACF and PACF for p and q order determination ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","series_for_acf_pacf = y.diff().dropna() if d_order == 1 else y\n","\n","# Set background to white for all plots\n","plt.figure(figsize=(14, 6), facecolor='white')\n","ax = plt.gca()\n","ax.set_facecolor('white')\n","plot_acf(series_for_acf_pacf, lags=40, ax=ax, title='Autocorrelation Function (ACF) of Stationary Close Price')\n","plt.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.6)\n","plt.show()\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(14, 6), facecolor='white')\n","ax = plt.gca()\n","ax.set_facecolor('white')\n","plot_pacf(series_for_acf_pacf, lags=40, ax=ax, title='Partial Autocorrelation Function (PACF) of Stationary Close Price')\n","plt.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.6)\n","plt.show()\n","\n","print(\"\\nInterpretation Guidance for ACF/PACF:\")\n","print(\"- **ACF (Autocorrelation Function):** Measures the correlation between the series and its lagged values.\")\n","print(\"  - If it dies down slowly, it suggests an AR component. If it cuts off sharply after 'q' lags, it suggests an MA(q) process.\")\n","print(\"- **PACF (Partial Autocorrelation Function):** Measures the correlation between the series and its lagged values, with the influence of intervening lags removed.\")\n","print(\"  - If it dies down slowly, it suggests an MA component. If it cuts off sharply after 'p' lags, it suggests an AR(p) process.\")\n","print(\"\\nBased on these plots, you'd select initial 'p' and 'q' values. For financial time series, patterns can be complex. Often, small values like p=1, q=1 or p=2, q=2 are good starting points.\")\n","\n","# Initial guess for p and q based on common practice or preliminary visual inspection.\n","p_order = 1 # Initial guess for AR order\n","q_order = 1 # Initial guess for MA order\n","\n","print(f\"\\nInitial chosen ARIMA orders: p={p_order}, d={d_order}, q={q_order}\")\n","\n","# --- Step 3: Split Data into Training and Testing Sets ---\n","train_size = int(len(df_arima_clean) * 0.8) # 80% for training\n","train_y = y.iloc[:train_size]\n","test_y = y.iloc[train_size:]\n","\n","train_X = X.iloc[:train_size]\n","test_X = X.iloc[train_size:]\n","\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_STEP}--- Step 3: Data Splitting ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","print(f\"Total data points for modeling: {len(df_arima_clean)}\")\n","print(f\"Training data points: {len(train_y)} (from {train_y.index.min()} to {train_y.index.max()})\")\n","print(f\"Testing data points: {len(test_y)} (from {test_y.index.min()} to {test_y.index.max()})\")\n","\n","# --- Step 4: Fit the ARIMAX Model ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_STEP}--- Step 4: Fitting ARIMAX Model ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","try:\n","    model = SARIMAX(\n","        train_y,\n","        exog=train_X,\n","        order=(p_order, d_order, q_order),\n","        enforce_stationarity=False,\n","        enforce_invertibility=False\n","    )\n","    # Added `maxiter` to potentially help with ConvergenceWarning\n","    model_fit = model.fit(disp=False, maxiter=1000)\n","\n","    print(\"\\nARIMAX Model Summary:\")\n","    print(model_fit.summary())\n","\n","except Exception as e:\n","    print(f\"\\nError occurred during model fitting: {e}\")\n","    print(\"Possible reasons: chosen (p,d,q) orders are not suitable, data issues, or multicollinearity in exog variables.\")\n","    print(\"Consider adjusting orders or simplifying the set of exogenous variables.\")\n","    raise\n","\n","# --- Step 5: Make Predictions on the Test Set ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_STEP}--- Step 5: Making Predictions on the Test Set ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","forecast_start_index = len(train_y)\n","forecast_end_index = len(df_arima_clean) - 1\n","\n","predictions = model_fit.predict(start=forecast_start_index,\n","                                end=forecast_end_index,\n","                                exog=test_X)\n","predictions.index = test_y.index\n","\n","print(\"Sample of Actual vs Predicted Values (First 5 from Test Set):\")\n","comparison_df = pd.DataFrame({'Actual': test_y, 'Predicted': predictions}).head()\n","print(comparison_df)\n","\n","# --- Step 6: Evaluate the Model's Performance ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_STEP}--- Step 6: Evaluating Model Performance ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","\n","actual_values_eval = test_y.dropna()\n","predicted_values_eval = predictions.loc[actual_values_eval.index].dropna()\n","\n","if len(actual_values_eval) != len(predicted_values_eval):\n","    print(\"Warning: Length mismatch after cleaning for evaluation. Aligning indices.\")\n","    common_index = actual_values_eval.index.intersection(predicted_values_eval.index)\n","    actual_values_eval = actual_values_eval.loc[common_index]\n","    predicted_values_eval = predicted_values_eval.loc[common_index]\n","\n","rmse = sqrt(mean_squared_error(actual_values_eval, predicted_values_eval))\n","mae = mean_absolute_error(actual_values_eval, predicted_values_eval)\n","\n","print(f\"Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE): {rmse:.4f}\")\n","print(f\"Mean Absolute Error (MAE): {mae:.4f}\")\n","\n","# --- ENHANCED PLOTTING (User Request) ---\n","plt.style.use('default') # Use default style to allow full control over background\n","\n","fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(18, 9), facecolor='white')\n","ax.set_facecolor('white') # Set axes background to white\n","\n","# Plot Training Data (Actual)\n","ax.plot(train_y.index, train_y, label='Training Data (Actual)', color=custom_plot_colors[0], linewidth=2.5, alpha=0.9)\n","\n","# Plot Test Data (Actual)\n","ax.plot(test_y.index, test_y, label='Test Data (Actual)', color=custom_plot_colors[1], linewidth=2.5, alpha=0.9)\n","\n","# Connect Training and Test Actuals with a subtle line\n","if not train_y.empty and not test_y.empty:\n","    ax.plot([train_y.index[-1], test_y.index[0]], [train_y.iloc[-1], test_y.iloc[0]],\n","            color=custom_plot_colors[1], linestyle=':', linewidth=1.5, alpha=0.6, label='_nolegend_')\n","\n","# Plot ARIMAX Predictions\n","ax.plot(predictions.index, predictions, label='ARIMAX Predictions (Test Set)', color=custom_plot_colors[2], linestyle='--', linewidth=2.5)\n","\n","\n","# --- Forecasting for the next 10 days (User Request) ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_TITLE}--- Forecasting Next 10 Days ---{COLOR_END}\") # Colored text\n","n_forecast_steps = 10 # Number of steps to forecast into the future\n","last_data_date = df_arima_clean.index.max()\n","future_dates = [last_data_date + datetime.timedelta(days=i) for i in range(1, n_forecast_steps + 1)]\n","\n","# Prepare future exogenous variables for forecasting.\n","# SARIMAX.forecast() will use the last observed value for non-differenced exogenous variables.\n","# For differenced variables (like Daily_Return), it will assume future values are 0 if not explicitly provided.\n","# Here, we explicitly assume future exogenous values are the same as the last observed values for simplicity.\n","# In a real application, you'd need to forecast these exogenous variables separately.\n","future_exog_data = np.tile(X.iloc[-1].values, (n_forecast_steps, 1))\n","future_exog_df = pd.DataFrame(future_exog_data, index=future_dates, columns=existing_exog_variables)\n","\n","print(f\"Forecasting from {future_dates[0].strftime('%Y-%m-%d')} to {future_dates[-1].strftime('%Y-%m-%d')}\")\n","print(\"NOTE: Future exogenous variables are assumed to be the same as the last observed values. This is a simplification and may impact accuracy.\")\n","\n","try:\n","    # Use the `forecast` method to predict future values\n","    future_forecast = model_fit.forecast(steps=n_forecast_steps, exog=future_exog_df)\n","    future_forecast.index = future_dates\n","    ax.plot(future_forecast.index, future_forecast, label=f'Forecasted Next {n_forecast_steps} Days', color=custom_plot_colors[3], linestyle='-', linewidth=2, alpha=0.8)\n","    print(\"Future Forecast (Next 10 Days):\")\n","    print(future_forecast)\n","except Exception as e:\n","    print(f\"Error during future forecasting: {e}\")\n","    print(\"This can happen if exog variables are not correctly handled or if the model struggles with extrapolation.\")\n","\n","\n","# Set Main Plot Title and Labels\n","ax.set_title('Bitcoin Close Price: ARIMAX Model Predictions and Forecast', fontsize=20, color='#333333', fontweight='bold')\n","ax.set_xlabel('Date', fontsize=16, color='#555555')\n","ax.set_ylabel('Close Price (USD)', fontsize=16, color='#555555')\n","ax.legend(fontsize=13, loc='upper left', frameon=True, shadow=True, fancybox=True)\n","ax.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.6)\n","\n","\n","# --- Inset Plot (Zoomed-in View for 2025 up to available data) ---\n","inset_ax = ax.inset_axes([0.15, 0.55, 0.40, 0.40], facecolor='white') # Set inset background to white\n","inset_ax.set_facecolor('white')\n","\n","# Define the zoom period (from beginning of 2025 to end of *available* data in 2025)\n","zoom_start_date_inset = pd.Timestamp(\"2025-01-01\")\n","zoom_end_date_inset = df_arima_clean.index.max() # Last date available in your dataset\n","\n","# Filter data for the zoomed region for actuals (from the full 'y' series)\n","zoom_actuals = y[(y.index >= zoom_start_date_inset) & (y.index <= zoom_end_date_inset)]\n","# Filter predictions for the zoomed region (from the 'predictions' series for test set)\n","zoom_predictions = predictions[(predictions.index >= zoom_start_date_inset) & (predictions.index <= zoom_end_date_inset)]\n","\n","# Plot in the inset\n","inset_ax.plot(zoom_actuals.index, zoom_actuals, label='Actuals (Zoom)', color=custom_plot_colors[1], linewidth=2.0)\n","inset_ax.plot(zoom_predictions.index, zoom_predictions, label='Predictions (Zoom)', color=custom_plot_colors[2], linestyle='--', linewidth=2.0)\n","\n","# Add any future forecast part that falls within the inset's date range\n","future_forecast_in_inset = future_forecast[(future_forecast.index >= zoom_start_date_inset) & (future_forecast.index <= zoom_end_date_inset)]\n","if not future_forecast_in_inset.empty:\n","    inset_ax.plot(future_forecast_in_inset.index, future_forecast_in_inset, label='Forecast (Zoom)', color=custom_plot_colors[3], linestyle=':', linewidth=2.0)\n","\n","\n","# Set inset title and labels (smaller font size for inset)\n","inset_ax.set_title(f'Zoom: {zoom_start_date_inset.year} - {zoom_end_date_inset.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\")}', fontsize=12, color='#333333')\n","inset_ax.legend(fontsize=8, loc='upper left')\n","inset_ax.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\n","\n","# Adjust x-axis limits for the inset\n","# Ensure the x-limit covers the entire forecasted period if it extends into the zoom.\n","inset_ax.set_xlim(zoom_start_date_inset, max(zoom_end_date_inset, future_forecast.index.max() if not future_forecast.empty else zoom_end_date_inset))\n","\n","# Automatically adjust y-axis limits based on data in the inset\n","all_data_for_zoom_y = pd.concat([zoom_actuals, zoom_predictions, future_forecast_in_inset]).dropna()\n","if not all_data_for_zoom_y.empty:\n","    min_y_zoom = all_data_for_zoom_y.min() * 0.98\n","    max_y_zoom = all_data_for_zoom_y.max() * 1.02\n","    inset_ax.set_ylim(min_y_zoom, max_y_zoom)\n","\n","\n","# Indicate the zoomed region on the main plot\n","ax.indicate_inset_zoom(inset_ax, edgecolor=\"black\", linewidth=1.5, linestyle=':')\n","\n","plt.tight_layout() # Adjust layout to prevent labels from overlapping\n","plt.show()\n","\n","# --- Step 7: Analyze Model Residuals ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_STEP}--- Step 7: Residual Analysis ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","residuals = model_fit.resid # Extract residuals from the fitted model\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(16, 6), facecolor='white')\n","ax = plt.gca()\n","ax.set_facecolor('white')\n","plt.plot(residuals.index, residuals, color=custom_plot_colors[0], alpha=0.7)\n","plt.title('ARIMAX Model Residuals Over Time', fontsize=18, color='#333333')\n","plt.xlabel('Date', fontsize=14, color='#555555')\n","plt.ylabel('Residual', fontsize=14, color='#555555')\n","plt.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.6)\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(14, 6), facecolor='white')\n","ax = plt.gca()\n","ax.set_facecolor('white')\n","plot_acf(residuals, lags=40, ax=ax, title='ACF of ARIMAX Residuals (Should be White Noise)')\n","plt.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.6)\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","plt.figure(figsize=(14, 6), facecolor='white')\n","ax = plt.gca()\n","ax.set_facecolor('white')\n","plot_pacf(residuals, lags=40, ax=ax, title='PACF of ARIMAX Residuals (Should be White Noise)')\n","plt.grid(True, linestyle='--', alpha=0.6)\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n","\n","ljung_box_results = acorr_ljungbox(residuals, lags=[10, 20, 30], return_df=True)\n","print(\"\\nLjung-Box Test Results for Residuals:\")\n","print(ljung_box_results)\n","print(\"\\nInterpretation of Ljung-Box Test:\")\n","print(\"- For each lag, if the 'lb_pvalue' is > 0.05, it suggests there's no significant remaining autocorrelation at that lag.\")\n","print(\"- If any 'lb_pvalue' is <= 0.05, it suggests that the residuals are NOT white noise, indicating the model could be improved.\")\n","\n","# --- Next Steps for Model Improvement (Iterative Refinement) ---\n","print(f\"\\n{COLOR_START_TITLE}--- Next Steps for ARIMAX Model Improvement (Iterative Refinement) ---{COLOR_END}\")\n","print(\"1. **Parameter Optimization (p, d, q):**\")\n","print(\"   - Re-evaluate ACF/PACF of residuals. If there are significant spikes outside the blue confidence bands, adjust `p` and `q` accordingly.\")\n","print(\"   - Use the AIC/BIC values from the `model_fit.summary()`: lower values are better. Try different (p, d, q) combinations.\")\n","print(\"   - Consider using `pmdarima.auto_arima` (a separate library) which automates the search for optimal (p, d, q) based on information criteria.\")\n","# Here's where `pmdarima.auto_arima` would be relevant.\n","# from pmdarima import auto_arima\n","# You would use auto_arima like this:\n","# optimal_model = auto_arima(train_y, exog=train_X,\n","#                            start_p=1, start_q=1,\n","#                            max_p=3, max_q=3,\n","#                            m=1, # No seasonality for daily data (m=1)\n","#                            d=d_order, # Use determined d_order\n","#                            seasonal=False,\n","#                            trace=True,\n","#                            error_action='ignore',\n","#                            suppress_warnings=True,\n","#                            stepwise=True)\n","# print(optimal_model.summary())\n","# You would then use optimal_model.order for p, d, q.\n","\n","print(\"2. **Exogenous Variable Selection:**\")\n","print(\"   - Experiment with different subsets of `existing_exog_variables`. Some variables might not be contributing much or might introduce noise.\")\n","print(\"   - If you detect multicollinearity (e.g., using VIF scores) among your exogenous variables, consider removing highly correlated ones or combining them.\")\n","# To calculate VIF scores, you would typically use statsmodels.regression.linear_model.OLS\n","# and then calculate VIF for each exogenous variable.\n","# Example VIF calculation (requires adding a constant to X):\n","# from statsmodels.stats.outliers_influence import variance_inflation_factor\n","# from statsmodels.tools.tools import add_constant\n","# X_const = add_constant(X)\n","# vif_data = pd.DataFrame()\n","# vif_data[\"feature\"] = X_const.columns\n","# vif_data[\"VIF\"] = [variance_inflation_factor(X_const.values, i) for i in range(X_const.shape[1])]\n","# print(vif_data)\n","# High VIF (e.g., > 5 or > 10) indicates high multicollinearity.\n","print(\"   - Explore interaction terms or polynomial terms for exogenous variables if they seem to have non-linear effects.\")\n","print(\"3. **Seasonality (SARIMAX):**\")\n","print(\"   - Although daily Bitcoin prices might not show strong daily/weekly seasonality, if you are modeling other time frequencies (e.g., hourly) or if you suspect yearly cycles for some assets, consider adding `seasonal_order=(P, D, Q, S)` to the `SARIMAX` model.\")\n","print(\"4. **Robust Validation:**\")\n","print(\"   - Implement **Rolling Forecasts** (also known as Walk-Forward Optimization): This is a more robust validation technique for time series where the model is re-trained or updated as new data becomes available. This simulates real-world forecasting more accurately.\")\n","print(\"5. **Error Analysis:**\")\n","print(\"   - Further investigate periods where the model's predictions significantly deviate from actual values. Are there specific market events or conditions during these periods that the model failed to capture?\")\n","print(\"6. **Model Comparison:**\")\n","print(\"   - Once you have a refined ARIMAX model, you can compare its performance (using RMSE, MAE, etc.) against other models like LSTM or Transformer-based models, which you mentioned you want to implement next.\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"d4c0f30d","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.202148,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:10.018934","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:09.816786","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff; line-height:25px\">\n","    \n","**Summary of Methodology**\n","\n","This section outlines the systematic approach undertaken for forecasting Bitcoin's \"Close\" price using the AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) model, augmented with exogenous variables (ARIMAX). The methodology emphasizes data-driven parameter selection and rigorous model validation, building upon prior comprehensive data preparation and exploratory data analysis.\n","\n","**1. Data Preparation and Feature Engineering**\n","\n","The initial phase involved acquiring historical Bitcoin price data. This dataset was then extensively processed to derive a suite of features crucial for time series analysis and forecasting:\n","* **Core Price Data:** \"Open,\" \"High,\" \"Low,\" and \"Close\" prices.\n","* **Volume:** Transaction volume, indicating market activity.\n","* **Daily Return:** Calculated as the percentage change in \"Close\" price, representing daily price movements.\n","* **Simple Moving Averages (SMAs):** Computed for various periods (e.g., 3, 7, 15, 30, 50 days) to identify trends.\n","* **Exponential Moving Averages (EMAs):** Calculated for corresponding periods, giving more weight to recent data.\n","* **MACD (Moving Average Convergence Divergence):** A momentum indicator showing the relationship between two moving averages of prices. This involves calculating 12-day EMA, 26-day EMA, the MACD line (12-day EMA - 26-day EMA), and a 9-day Signal line (9-day EMA of the MACD line).\n","* **RSI (Relative Strength Index):** A momentum oscillator measuring the speed and change of price movements, calculated over a 14-day period.\n","* **Bollinger Bands:** Comprising a Middle Band (SMA), Upper Band (SMA + standard deviations), and Lower Band (SMA - standard deviations), providing insights into volatility and potential price envelopes.\n","\n","Following feature creation, the DataFrame was cleaned by removing initial `NaN` values resulting from rolling window calculations, ensuring a complete and valid dataset for model training. The 'Date' column was set as the DataFrame index and localized to be timezone-naive for compatibility with the `statsmodels` library. A copy of the main DataFrame (`df_arima_modeling`) was created to ensure the original `df` remains pristine for subsequent modeling approaches (e.g., LSTM, Transformer).\n","\n","**2. ARIMAX Model Specification**\n","\n","The ARIMAX model, an extension of ARIMA, incorporates exogenous variables to enhance predictive power. Its specification involves three core non-seasonal parameters $(p, d, q)$ and the selection of relevant exogenous predictors.\n","\n","* **2.1. Determining the Integrated Order ($d$): Stationarity Testing**\n","    ARIMA models require the time series to be stationary, meaning its statistical properties (mean, variance, and autocorrelation structure) remain constant over time. Financial price series are typically non-stationary. The **Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test** was employed to formally assess the stationarity of the \"Close\" price series.\n","    * If the p-value from the ADF test is greater than a chosen significance level (e.g., 0.05), the null hypothesis of non-stationarity cannot be rejected, indicating the need for differencing.\n","    * For Bitcoin's \"Close\" price, a first-order differencing ($d=1$) is commonly applied, transforming the price series into a return series (similar to 'Daily_Return') which often exhibits stationarity. The ADF test was re-applied to the differenced series to confirm stationarity.\n","\n","* **2.2. Identifying Autoregressive ($p$) and Moving Average ($q$) Orders**\n","    Once stationarity is achieved, the orders for the AutoRegressive ($p$) and Moving Average ($q$) components are determined through the analysis of the **Autocorrelation Function (ACF)** and **Partial Autocorrelation Function (PACF)** plots of the stationary series.\n","    * The **ACF** plot reveals the correlation between the series and its lagged values. A sharp drop-off in the ACF after a certain number of lags often suggests the order of the MA component ($q$).\n","    * The **PACF** plot displays the correlation between the series and its lagged values, after removing the effects of intermediate lags. A sharp drop-off in the PACF after a certain number of lags often indicates the order of the AR component ($p$).\n","    * Initial orders of $(p=1, q=1)$ are typically chosen as a starting point for financial time series, given their often complex correlation structures, and are subject to refinement.\n","\n","* **2.3. Exogenous Variable Selection**\n","    The correlation heatmap guided the selection of exogenous variables. Variables highly correlated with the \"Close\" price, but not directly redundant or perfectly collinear, were considered.\n","    * **'Daily_Return' and 'Volume'** were identified as strong candidates due to their direct market relevance and high correlations.\n","    * **'MACD', 'Signal', 'RSI', 'Middle_Band', 'Upper_Band', and 'Lower_Band'** were also included. While these are derived from price, their transformation and distinct informational content (momentum, volatility, trend envelopes) allow them to serve as valuable exogenous inputs. Direct raw SMA/EMA values were avoided to mitigate potential multicollinearity.\n","\n","**3. Model Training and Validation**\n","\n","The prepared dataset was chronologically split into training (80%) and testing (20%) sets to simulate real-world forecasting scenarios and prevent data leakage. The `SARIMAX` model from `statsmodels` was then fitted to the training data, incorporating the selected endogenous series and exogenous variables, using the determined $(p, d, q)$ orders.\n","\n","**4. Prediction and Performance Evaluation**\n","\n","Upon successful model fitting, predictions were generated for the test set. Model performance was quantified using standard error metrics:\n","* **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE):** Measures the average magnitude of the errors, penalizing larger errors more significantly.\n","* **Mean Absolute Error (MAE):** Represents the average absolute magnitude of the errors, providing a robust measure less sensitive to outliers.\n","A visual comparison of actual versus predicted \"Close\" prices was generated, highlighting the model's ability to track trends and predict values within the test period.\n","\n","**5. Residual Analysis**\n","\n","A critical step in validating time series models is the analysis of residuals (the differences between actual and predicted values).\n","* Residuals were plotted over time to visually inspect for any discernible patterns. Ideally, residuals should resemble white noise (i.e., be random, uncorrelated, and centered around zero).\n","* ACF and PACF plots of the residuals were examined to detect any remaining autocorrelation. The absence of significant spikes beyond confidence intervals indicates that the model has adequately captured the underlying time series patterns.\n","* The **Ljung-Box test** was performed to statistically confirm whether the residuals exhibit white noise characteristics. A high p-value (e.g., > 0.05) for all lags suggests that the residuals are indeed white noise, implying a well-specified model. Conversely, low p-values indicate uncaptured patterns, necessitating further model refinement.\n","\n","**6. Future Forecasting**\n","\n","To provide a practical forward-looking insight, the model was used to forecast Bitcoin's \"Close\" price for the next 10 days beyond the available historical data. For this future forecast, a simplifying assumption was made for the exogenous variables: their values for the forecast horizon were assumed to be the same as their last observed values. It is important to note that the accuracy of such forecasts heavily relies on the validity of this assumption, as future exogenous variable values are generally unknown and would ideally require their own forecasting.\n","\n","This comprehensive approach allows for a robust development and evaluation of the ARIMAX model, laying the groundwork for further iterative refinement and comparative analysis with advanced deep learning models."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e0ffb49d","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.196622,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:10.416304","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:10.219682","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<a id=\"LSTM\"></a>\n","\n","<h1 style=\" \n","    background-color:#cddcdf; \n","    background-position: center;\n","    font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\n","    font-size: 1.5em;\n","    color: Black;\n","    text-align: center;\n","    padding: 15px;\n","    border-radius: 5px 10px;\n","\">\n","    LSTM\n","</h1>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"0593f4cc","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.198064,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:10.812784","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:10.61472","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<div style=\"border: 2px solid #264653; padding: 15px; background-color: #ffffff; line-height:25px\">\n","    \n","**Unveiling the Power of Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) Networks in Financial Time Series Forecasting**\n","\n","In the relentlessly complex and often unpredictable realm of financial markets, the pursuit of accurate time series forecasting remains a central, albeit formidable, challenge. Traditional statistical models, while foundational, frequently grapple with the inherent non-linearity, high-dimensionality, and the elusive long-range dependencies that characterize real-world financial data. This persistent gap in predictive capability necessitates a foray into advanced deep learning architectures, where **Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks** have emerged as a singularly powerful paradigm, revolutionizing sequential data modeling and offering unprecedented insights into market dynamics.\n","\n","Our analytical journey begins with a crucial preliminary diagnostic: a **meticulously filtered correlation heatmap**. This is far more than a mere visual artifact; it serves as a critical initial compass, systematically quantifying **Pearson correlation coefficients** that satisfy a robust pre-defined threshold (e.g., $\\ge 0.25$), meticulously complemented by indicators of statistical significance (conventionally denoted by three asterisks, signifying a p-value less than 0.001). This powerful visualization unravels the intricate tapestry of interrelationships among a diverse array of financial variables. From the foundational price metrics—\"Close,\" \"Open,\" \"High,\" and \"Low\"—to \"Volume,\" \"Daily\\_Return,\" and an expansive suite of technical indicators such as Simple Moving Averages (SMAs), Exponential Moving Averages (EMAs) across various lookback periods (e.g., \"SMA3,\" \"SMA7,\" ..., \"SMA50,\" and \"EMA3,\" \"EMA7,\" ..., \"EMA50\"), and even components of Bollinger Bands (\"Middle\\_Band,\" \"Upper\\_Band\") which offer crucial insights into market volatility and trend envelopes—every highlighted correlation acts as a pivotal clue, profoundly informing and guiding our subsequent LSTM modeling strategy.\n","\n","Our overarching objective is unequivocal: to construct predictive models of unparalleled robustness and generalization capability for these dynamic financial time series. While our methodical approach acknowledges and, in some cases, precedes with established statistical techniques like ARIMA, the true quantum leap in predictive prowess is realized through the sophisticated architecture of LSTM networks.\n","\n","**Why LSTMs Are Indispensable for Financial Time Series Forecasting**\n","\n","Financial data is notoriously complex: it is inherently sequential, highly dynamic, influenced by a myriad of interconnected global and local factors, and frequently exhibits chaotic, non-linear, and non-stationary behavior. Traditional models often falter when confronted with such intrinsic complexities. This is precisely where the unique strengths of LSTMs become not just advantageous, but indispensable:\n","\n","* **Addressing the Vanishing/Exploding Gradient Problem:** Standard Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) suffer from the vanishing or exploding gradient problem, which severely impedes their ability to learn long-term dependencies. As gradients propagate back through many time steps, they either shrink exponentially (vanishing), making it impossible for the network to update weights for earlier inputs, or grow exponentially (exploding), leading to unstable training. LSTMs are meticulously engineered to circumvent this fundamental limitation through their innovative **gating mechanism**. This allows LSTMs to effectively **retain crucial information over extended periods** and selectively forget irrelevant past data, making them ideal for capturing long-term memory in financial sequences.\n","\n","* **Capturing Complex Non-Linear Patterns:** Financial markets are not governed by simple linear relationships. Price movements, volume spikes, and indicator crossovers interact in highly non-linear ways. LSTMs, as non-linear function approximators, can inherently **learn and model these intricate, non-linear relationships** directly from the raw sequential data. They don't require explicit feature engineering for every potential interaction or transformation, unlike many statistical models. The complex interplay between different technical indicators (as revealed by our heatmap), price actions, and volume can be learned by the network's internal states.\n","\n","* **Understanding Temporal Dependencies (Memory Cells):** The core innovation of LSTMs lies in their **memory cell state** ($C_t$) and three specialized **gate mechanisms**:\n","    * **Forget Gate ($f_t$):** This gate determines what information from the previous cell state ($C_{t-1}$) should be discarded. It uses a sigmoid function operating on the current input ($x_t$) and the previous hidden state ($h_{t-1}$). This is crucial for financial data where older, irrelevant information (e.g., specific market noise from months ago) might need to be 'forgotten' to focus on more recent, relevant trends.\n","    * **Input Gate ($i_t$):** This gate decides what new information from the current input ($x_t$) should be stored in the cell state. It has two parts: a sigmoid layer that decides which values to update, and a tanh layer that creates a vector of new candidate values ($\\tilde{C}_t$). This allows the LSTM to incorporate new market data efficiently.\n","    * **Output Gate ($o_t$):** This gate determines what part of the cell state ($C_t$) should be outputted as the new hidden state ($h_t$). It uses a sigmoid function to filter the cell state and a tanh function to scale the output. The hidden state then serves as the input to the next time step and as the basis for predictions.\n","    This sophisticated gating mechanism grants LSTMs the unparalleled ability to **selectively propagate, update, and output information**, enabling them to effectively manage the \"memory\" of the market across extensive time horizons, distinguishing relevant signals from noise.\n","\n","* **Handling Multi-Variate Inputs and Feature Interactions:** Our correlation heatmap reveals that various financial features are interconnected and jointly influence price movements. LSTMs are perfectly suited for **multi-variate time series forecasting**, where multiple input features (e.g., \"Close\", \"Volume\", \"SMA\", \"EMA\", \"RSI\") at each time step are fed into the network. The LSTM layers can learn complex, dynamic interactions *between* these features *over time*, capturing how, for instance, a surge in volume coinciding with an EMA crossover might signal a strong trend. This is a significant advantage over univariate models which can only consider the target series' past values.\n","\n","**The Deep Learning Forecasting Pipeline: Essential Stages for Robust LSTM Models**\n","\n","Building and deploying effective LSTM models for financial time series is a systematic, iterative, and often intricate process. Meticulous attention at each stage is paramount to ensure optimal predictive power, reliability, and generalization capability in real-world market scenarios:\n","\n","1.  **Comprehensive Data Acquisition & Meticulous Preparation:**\n","    This foundational stage is the bedrock of any successful deep learning initiative. It extends beyond merely gathering raw time series data for all variables highlighted by our correlation heatmap. It encompasses ensuring **impeccable data quality**: validating that data is meticulously **sorted in chronological order** and precisely time-indexed. Crucially, **missing values must be robustly handled**; this often involves sophisticated imputation strategies (e.g., linear interpolation, forward/backward fill, or more advanced methods like K-Nearest Neighbors imputation), or, in specific scenarios, careful selective exclusion, as even minor data gaps can significantly impede deep learning models. Furthermore, for deep learning models to converge efficiently and effectively during training, all numerical features must undergo essential **scaling**. Common techniques include `MinMaxScaler` (normalizing to a range like [-1, 1] or [0, 1]) or `StandardScaler` (transforming data to have a zero mean and unit variance). This normalization prevents features with larger magnitudes from disproportionately influencing the learning process. The final, critical transformation is converting the continuous time series data into a format suitable for LSTM networks: **sequential windows**. This involves creating fixed-length sequences (e.g., `seq_len` past timesteps) of our multi-variate features. Each such sequence serves as an input $X$ to the LSTM, where the goal is typically to predict the target \"Close\" price $Y$ at a future time step (e.g., the very next step after the sequence ends). This structured input representation is fundamental for LSTMs to learn and exploit intricate temporal patterns.\n","\n","2.  **Sophisticated Model Architecture Design & Configuration:**\n","    This stage is where the \"intelligence\" of our forecasting system is meticulously engineered. For LSTM models, this translates to carefully designing the network's internal structure:\n","    * **Number of LSTM Layers:** Deciding on a single or **stacked LSTM layers**. Stacking multiple LSTM layers allows the network to learn hierarchical representations of the input sequence, where lower layers capture basic features and higher layers synthesize more abstract temporal patterns.\n","    * **Units (Neurons) Per Layer:** Determining the optimal number of LSTM units (neurons) within each layer. This directly influences the model's capacity to learn complex relationships; too few might lead to underfitting, while too many can cause overfitting and increased computational cost.\n","    * **`return_sequences` Parameter:** Setting `return_sequences=True` for intermediate LSTM layers when stacking, ensuring that the output of each LSTM layer is a sequence, which can then be fed as input to the subsequent LSTM layer. The final LSTM layer typically has `return_sequences=False` if followed by a `Dense` layer for a single-point prediction.\n","    * **Regularization with Dropout:** Strategically incorporating **Dropout layers** after LSTM layers. Dropout is a powerful regularization technique where a fraction of neurons are randomly \"dropped out\" (set to zero) during each training step. This prevents complex co-adaptations on the training data, forcing the network to learn more robust and generalized features, thereby significantly mitigating overfitting—a common challenge in deep learning models trained on financial data.\n","    * **Dense Output Layers:** Concluding the network with one or more **Dense (fully connected) layers** to transform the output from the final LSTM layer into the desired prediction format (e.g., a single numerical value for the forecasted \"Close\" price). The activation function for the output layer will typically be linear for regression tasks. The specific architectural choices profoundly impact the model's capacity to learn intricate patterns, its generalization ability, and its computational demands.\n","\n","3.  **Rigorous Model Training & Convergence Optimization:**\n","    With our meticulously prepared data and a precisely defined LSTM architecture, the model embarks on its iterative learning phase. This involves feeding the training data through the network, calculating the prediction error, and subsequently adjusting the model's internal weights and biases to minimize a chosen **loss function** (e.g., **Mean Squared Error (MSE)** or Mean Absolute Error (MAE) for regression tasks). The training is performed on a dedicated training dataset, while a separate, unseen **validation dataset** is crucial. This validation set provides an unbiased estimate of the model's generalization performance *during* training, acting as an early warning system for overfitting. To optimize the training process and ensure robust model convergence, we employ sophisticated training strategies:\n","    * **Optimizers:** Utilizing adaptive optimizers such as **Adam** (Adaptive Moment Estimation) or RMSprop, which dynamically adjust the learning rate for each parameter, leading to faster and more stable convergence.\n","    * **Early Stopping:** This vital callback monitors the model's performance on the validation set. If the validation loss does not improve for a specified number of `patience` epochs, training is automatically halted. This mechanism is critical for preventing overfitting by stopping training at the point where the model generalizes best to unseen data, thereby saving significant computational resources.\n","    * **ReduceLROnPlateau:** This callback dynamically adjusts the **learning rate** during training. If the validation loss plateaus (i.e., does not improve) for a certain number of epochs, the learning rate is reduced by a specified `factor`. This helps the model escape local minima and converge more effectively to a better solution, particularly in the latter stages of training. The judicious selection of hyperparameters like `epochs`, `batch_size`, and the specifics of these callbacks are vital for achieving optimal training outcomes.\n","\n","4.  **Comprehensive Model Evaluation & Strategic Optimization:**\n","    Once the training phase is complete, the model's true predictive power and generalization capability are rigorously assessed on an entirely **unseen test dataset**. This separate dataset ensures an unbiased evaluation of how well the model performs on new, never-before-seen market conditions. Performance is quantified using widely accepted metrics, critically calculated on the *original scale* of the data (after inverse scaling the predictions):\n","    * **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE):** A frequently used measure of the differences between values predicted by a model or an estimator and the values observed. RMSE is a good measure of accuracy, but only to compare prediction errors of different models or model configurations for a particular variable and not between variables. It penalizes larger errors more heavily.\n","    * **Mean Absolute Error (MAE):** Represents the average of the absolute differences between prediction and actual observation over the test set. MAE is a measure of errors between paired observations expressing the same phenomenon. It is easier to interpret than RMSE as it is directly in the units of the target variable.\n","    * **Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE):** Expresses the accuracy as a percentage of the error. It is particularly useful for comparing forecasts across different time series because it is scale-independent.\n","    This comprehensive evaluation provides deep insights into the model's strengths and weaknesses. Based on these insights, an iterative process of **hyperparameter tuning** (meticulously adjusting parameters like sequence length, batch size, learning rate schedules, and dropout rates) and model refinement is undertaken. This often involves systematic exploration techniques like **grid search**, **random search**, or more advanced **Bayesian optimization** to efficiently navigate the vast hyperparameter space and identify the configurations that yield consistently superior accuracy, stability, and robustness.\n","\n","5.  **Robust Forecasting & Strategic Deployment:**\n","    The culmination of our meticulous efforts is the ability to generate reliable future predictions. For financial time series, this typically involves **multi-step forecasting**, where the trained and validated LSTM model, having internalized historical patterns and relationships, predicts the next immediate time step. Crucially, this prediction is then fed back into the model as part of the input sequence to forecast the subsequent step, and so on. This recursive approach allows us to extend our insights into the future market landscape, providing crucial, data-driven intelligence for strategic decision-making. Depending on the specific application, these highly accurate forecasts can then be seamlessly integrated into a variety of real-world systems, including automated trading algorithms, sophisticated risk management frameworks, dynamic portfolio optimization tools, or strategic long-term investment planning platforms. The ability to visualize these forecasts alongside actual historical data (often in candlestick charts or line plots) is paramount for practical interpretation and validation.\n","\n","By meticulously executing each of these advanced stages, and by harnessing the unparalleled capabilities of Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks—models uniquely suited to unravel the complex, non-linear, and time-dependent patterns embedded within financial data—we aim to construct highly accurate, robust, and actionable forecasting solutions that fundamentally transcend the limitations of traditional methodologies."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"83f46afa","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.199677,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:11.211699","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:11.012022","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<h1 style=\" \n","    background-color:#F9F9F7; \n","    background-position: center;\n","    font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\n","    font-size: 1.5em;\n","    color: Black;\n","    text-align: center;\n","    padding: 15px;\n","    border-radius: 5px 10px;\n","\">\n","    TENSORFLOW\n","</h1>"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":39,"id":"1b5176e4","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2025-10-21T10:46:11.612341Z","iopub.status.busy":"2025-10-21T10:46:11.612041Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-10-21T10:46:32.650536Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-10-21T10:46:32.649689Z"},"papermill":{"duration":21.242888,"end_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:32.653595","exception":false,"start_time":"2025-10-21T10:46:11.410707","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Original df 'Date' column timezone: UTC\n","Shape of your loaded df: (4053, 24)\n","\u001b[96m--- LSTM Model 1: Close Price Forecasting with All Indicators ---\u001b[0m\n","\u001b[96mTarget: Only 'Close' Price\u001b[0m\n","\n","\u001b[92m--- Step A: Data Preparation for LSTM ---\u001b[0m\n","Deep copy 'df_lstm_1' created. Original 'df' remains untouched for future models.\n","Removed 14 rows with NaNs from selected features. New shape: (4039, 15)\n","Scaled feature data shape (df_lstm_1_values): (4039, 14)\n","Sequence data shape: (4020,